\begin{document}

\title{Detecting AI-Generated Vietnamese Text:\\
A Controlled Comparison of PhoBERT\\ and BamiBERT as Feature Extractors\\ for CNN and BiLSTM}

\author{\IEEEauthorblockN{Nguyen Ho Giang Long}
\IEEEauthorblockA{Faculty of Information Technology\\
Ho Chi Minh City Open University\\
Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam\\
Email: 2351010115long@ou.edu.vn}}

\maketitle

% =====================================================================
\begin{abstract}
The widespread use of large language models (LLMs) has made it increasingly difficult to distinguish the text written by human and generated by AI, especially when the text written by AI from existing text from human. This study conducts a comparison of controlled of two Vietnamese pre-trained models, PhoBERT and BamiBERT, as frozen feature extractors, with two classification models (heads), CNN and BiLSTM. I constructed a dataset of 5000 information technology papers, including text written by human, generated by LLM and human-text written by LLM. To ensure two encoders process the same amount of content, all text were truncated to the first 225 syllables before being fed into each model's tokenizer. Through 5-fold cross-validation, BamiBERT + CNN archived the best results with an Accuracy of 95.42\% and an F1 score of 95.33\%. BamiBERT outperformed PhoBERT by only about 0.6 percentage points of an Accuracy using CNN, smaller than the standard deviation across the folds, while CNN outperformed BiLSTM by about 4 percentage points with two encoders. All of BamiBERT's advantages are focused on the AI-rewritten text group (87.52\% compared with 84.56\%), with a computational cost roughly double that of PhoBERT. The parallel combination of CNN and BiLSTM did not improve upon the performance of the only CNN (95.02\%).      
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
AI-generated text detection, Vietnamese, PhoBERT, BamiBERT, CNN, BiLSTM, frozen features
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% =====================================================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Nowadays, Large language models (LLMs) capable of generating fluent, grammatically correct text with a style closely resembling with human-written. This brings a lot of benefit in education, communication and content production, it also increases the risk of the unverified news, plagiarism, and misinformation [1]. Therefore, AI-generated text systems are now receiving significant research concerned [1], [2].

For the Vietnamese language, the number of research is still very small and primarily relies on essay datasets. Additionally, a real-world but more challenging scenario involves text originally written by a human and then rewritten by an LLM: the content remains the same, but the phrasing changes.

Theocharopoulos et al [8] compared various text representation on English scientific abstracts and found out frozen BERT archived the results much smaller than fine-tuned (accuracy 0.834 vs 0.987). With Vietnamese, Tran et al [10] introduced ViDetect, an essay of student dataset with AI text created by rewritten from original text, and evaluated five pre-trained models, including PhoBERT.

For Vietnamese, PhoBERT \cite{phobert} is a widely used  pre-trained model, but it requires text must be word segmentation before fed into the model. BamiBERT \cite{bamibert} is a newer model that directly accepts raw text and is reported by the authors to outperform PhoBERT on most evaluation tasks. However, it is not sure if this advantage holds when applied to the problem of detecting AI-generated text. Furthermore, when comparing two models with two different tokenizers, cutting the same amount of tokens may cause the two models to read different amounts of content, leading to skew the comparison results.

The main purpose of this study is evaluating and comparing the performance of PhoBERT and BamiBERT, using as the frozen feature extractors, along with two classification models, CNN and BiLSTM in detecting AI-generated text, where the models share the same dataset, inputs and training process. The main contribution including:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A dataset of 5\,000 Vietnamese language of IT news articles including three types: human-written, AI-generated, and AI-rewritten from human-rewritten articles.
  \item A controlled comparison setup: two frozen encoders read the first 225 syllables of each article, using the same classification model, the same data splitting method, and the same hyperparameters.
  \item Evaluation of four encoder-head combinations using 5-fold cross-validation, analysis by each type of text, and computational cost.
\end{itemize}

% =====================================================================
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Detection of LLM-Generated Text}
Detecting text generated by LLMs is considered as a typical classification problem, in which a model assign a label, human or AI to an input text. The current methods are categorized into two groups [1]: Active category, embedding the watermark into the text at the time of generation, and passive category only rely on content and features of the text. This study belongs to passive category, which includes two main approaches: \emph{zero-shot}, which doesn't require training classification models, and \emph{supervised learning}, training classification model on labeled text. A survey [2] notes that supervised detectors heavily rely on labeled dataset and easily lose performance when transferred to different data domains.  


\textbf{Zero-shot.} DetectGPT [3] is based on the hypothesis that text generated by a model lies in a region that has negative log-likelihood: when that text is slightly changed, the log-likelihood typically decreases, whereas this is not the case for human-written text. The method covers 15\% of the words (in two-word segmentation) and then filling the gaps by the T5 model to generate noise versions. After that, calculating the difference between the log-likelihood of the original text and its noise version (normalized by the standard deviation) and comparing with a threshold; log-likelihood is calculated by the source model so it needs to get access to this model. The authors evaluated on XSum news, the SQuAD academic paper corpus, and the WritingPrompts story corpus, with the texts generated by the models (GPT-2 with 1.5B parameters to GPT-NeoX with 20B parameters), as well as GPT-3. The news generated by GPT-NeoX, the AUROC increased from 0.81 of strongest zero-shot method to 0.95, and DetectGPT performed best in 14 out of 15 data-model combinations (an average increase of 0.06 AUROC). Compared to the supervised RoBERTa detector, DetectGPT performs equivalently on English news but the supervised detector performs worse on scientific texts and fails completely in German. A limitation of this method is needed to access the souce model's probability and must generate and score multiple noise versions for each text, making it computationally cost intensive. This approach does not require labeled data, differing from supervised learning approach used in this study. 

\textbf{Supervised learning with a pre-trained model.} Guo et al.~\cite{guo2023hc3} constructed HC3, a dataset comparing human and ChatGPT responses to the same question. The English section contains 24,322 questions, 58,546 human responses, and 26,903 ChatGPT responses, sourced from Reddit ELI5, WikiQA, Wikipedia, medicine, and finance; the Chinese section contains 12,853 questions, 22,259 human responses, and 17,522 ChatGPT responses. They compared logistic regression on GLTR features with a fine-tuned RoBERTa model, evaluating performance on full text and on individual sentences. When shifting from full text to the sentence level, RoBERTa's F1 score decreased by only 1.5--2\% for English, while GLTR's decreased by more than 10\%, indicating that the detection method based on the pre-trained model is more robust than the manually crafted features.

Theocharopoulos et al.~\cite{theocharopoulos} constructed a dataset from 14,331 articles in the CORD-19 repository (scientific texts on COVID-19), each with an English title and abstract. For each title, the authors used GPT-3 (the Da Vinci model) to generate an additional abstract, resulting in a final dataset of 28,662 samples---half written by humans and half generated by GPT-3---split 80\%/20\% (22,930 training samples and 5,732 test samples, with both versions of the same title assigned to the same side). They compared various text representations combined with machine learning models: TF-IDF with Logistic Regression, Naive Bayes, and SVM; LSTM with Word2Vec; BERT~\cite{bert} with fixed weights (only the fully connected layer was trained); fine-tuned BERT; and LSTM applied to BERT features. The results are averages across 100 independent runs, with early stopping based on validation loss. SVM and Logistic Regression on TF-IDF achieved accuracies of 0.980 and 0.975, respectively; fine-tuned BERT and LSTM with Word2Vec both achieved 0.987; while BERT with fixed weights achieved only 0.834, and the LSTM on BERT features achieved only 0.697. This work is the closest to my research because it uses features from a pre-trained model in a frozen state, combined with a classification model placed downstream, and demonstrates that performance depends heavily on that classification model.

\textbf{Robustness to rewritten text.} Krishna et al.~\cite{krishna2023dipper} studied the robustness of pre-trained detectors against rewritten text, which aligns with my AI-Rewritten text category. They generated 300-token texts using GPT-2-XL, OPT-13B, and text-davinci-003 for two tasks: continuing text from two opening sentences taken from WikiText-103 and answering long-form questions. They trained DIPPER, an 11-billion-parameter rewriting model (fine-tuned from T5-XXL on 6.3 million rephrasing pairs), to rewrite these texts and then re-evaluated five detectors (watermark, DetectGPT, GPTZero, OpenAI's classifier, and RankGen) at a 1\% false positive rate. Rewriting caused a significant drop in accuracy: watermark dropped from 100\% to 57.2\%, DetectGPT from 70.3\% to 4.6\%, and OpenAI's classifier from 30.0\% to 15.6\% with GPT-3.5. They propose a defense mechanism that searches a repository of 15 million generated texts and detects 80.4\% to 97.3\% of rewritten texts, but this approach requires the model provider to maintain a text repository.

\subsection{Detection of Vietnamese Text}
Tran et al.~\cite{videtect} introduced ViDetect, the first dataset for AI-generated text detection in Vietnamese, comprising 6,800 essays, of which 3,400 were written by humans. The human-written essays are sample compositions by students at various grade levels, collected from Vietnamese educational websites, published before 2021, and at least 256 words long; the AI-generated portions were created by having ChatGPT 3.5 and ChatGPT 4.0 to rewrite these essays while preserving the main ideas. The data was split in a 7:1:2 ratio for training, validation, and testing. The authors fine-tuned and evaluated five models (ViT5, BARTpho, PhoBERT, mDeBERTa-v3, and mBERT) with input sizes of 64, 128, and 256 tokens, using Accuracy and F1 scores. The average accuracy of the five models was 0.8580, 0.8721, and 0.8437 for the three input lengths, respectively; PhoBERT-base achieved 0.8743, 0.8819, and 0.8708, while the best result for a single model was approximately 0.90 (mDeBERTa-v3 with 64 tokens and mBERT with 128 tokens). Increasing the input length did not improve model performance. This work differs from my research in that it uses student essays, relies solely on AI-rewritten text, fine-tunes the entire model, and does not include BamiBERT.

