\subsection{Overview}
Figure~\ref{fig:overview} illustrates the overall workflow. The scope of this study is to classify Vietnamese text as either human-written or generated with the involvement of an LLM. The four models compared are combinations of two encoders (PhoBERT, BamiBERT) and two classification models (CNN, BiLSTM). All other factors---including the data, dataset splitting, input content, seed, optimization parameters, and number of epochs---were kept constant, so that differences in results stem solely from the encoders and classification models.

\subsection{Data Collection}
The dataset consists of articles in the fields of information technology and electronics, collected from six Vietnamese-language news sites: ICTnews (VietNamNet), Quan Tri Mang, Thanh Nien, VnExpress, GenK, and Tuoi Tre. The dataset contains three types of texts:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Human}: original articles written by human authors.
  \item \textbf{AI-Generated}: articles generated from scratch by an LLM, using a prompt with a specific topic and a request for a writing style similar to that of a human writer.
  \item \textbf{AI-Rewritten}: Original articles rewritten by an LLM.
\end{itemize}
The AI-generated and rewritten texts were produced using two LLMs---Gemini (Google) and DeepSeek---via their free chat interfaces. Since the free versions may be modified by the providers over time, I cannot precisely identify the specific model versions at the time the data was generated. The two types of AI are labeled 1, while the human-written articles are labeled 0. The dataset is balanced between the two classes, consisting of 2,500 human-written articles, 1,250 AI-generated articles, and 1,250 AI-rewritten articles (Table~\ref{tab:dataset}).

Of the 2,500 human-written articles, 2,270 (90.8\%) were published in 2026, with the remainder spread out from 2010 to 2025.

