\subsection{Preprocessing}
The text was cleaned in three steps:
\begin{itemize}
  \item removing URLs;
  \item removing HTML tags;
  \item standardizing whitespace (between words, at the beginning and end of the text).
\end{itemize}
I do not remove punctuation, special characters, or symbols, as is common in many other NLP tasks, because the use of these elements is a key signal for distinguishing human-written text from AI-generated text.

% NOTE: the next heading is a machine-translation wording of "truncating the text by the number of syllables".
\textbf{Split the text by the number of syllables.} The two encoders use different tokenizers, so the same number of tokens corresponds to different amounts of content. In initial testing with the same 256-token limit, PhoBERT processed approximately 40\% more content than BamiBERT, because each PhoBERT token is typically a complete compound word. To eliminate this source of variation, all text was truncated to the first 225 syllables before tokenization, ensuring that both encoders always received the same text segment. The value of 225 was chosen so that nearly all of PhoBERT's input falls within the 256-token limit (99.58\% of the articles). With 225 syllables, BamiBERT requires a maximum of 521 tokens (99th percentile is 358), so BamiBERT's 512-token limit adds virtually no additional content. Since 98.3\% of the texts are at least 225 syllables long (100\% of human-written texts, approximately 96.6--96.7\% of AI-generated texts), this truncation also virtually eliminates the length discrepancy between human-written and AI-generated texts, preventing the model from learning based on length rather than writing style.

\subsection{Text Representation}
I used two pre-trained Vietnamese models as backbones: PhoBERT-base~\cite{phobert} and BamiBERT~\cite{bamibert}. Each backbone consists of a corresponding tokenizer and encoder, and the two backbones differ in their input preparation steps. PhoBERT was pre-trained on tokenized text, so the input is word-segmented using the underthesea library~\cite{underthesea} before tokenization; the syllables of a word are joined by underscores and treated as a single unit (e.g., \emph{trí\_tuệ nhân\_tạo}). BamiBERT accepts raw text and tokenizes it directly using its BPE vocabulary. The length limit is $L = 256$ tokens for PhoBERT and $L = 512$ tokens for BamiBERT.

Given a token sequence of length $L$, the encoder generates a sequence of context-hidden states at the final layer $\mathbf{H} \in \mathbb{R}^{L \times d}$, where $d = 768$ is the hidden dimension of both encoders. The encoder is \textbf{frozen}: it remains in evaluation mode (no dropout) and does not compute gradients, so $\mathbf{H}$ for each text is fixed. Therefore, $\mathbf{H}$ only needs to be computed once for the entire dataset and is saved (in fp16 format) for shared use across both classification models and all folds.

\subsection{Classification Models}
% NOTE: machine-translation wording to revisit: "buffer/filler positions" = padding positions, "dimensions" = directions.
The two classification models (heads) both receive $\mathbf{H}$ and a mask $\mathbf{m} \in \{0,1\}^{L}$ mark true tokens ($m_i = 1$) and padding tokens ($m_i = 0$), then return a probability distribution over the two classes. They differ only in how they aggregate $\mathbf{H}$ into a fixed-size vector $\mathbf{z}$. The heads are initialized randomly and are the only components that are trained. Filler positions that carry no content are excluded from the aggregation.

\textbf{CNN.} According to Kim~\cite{kim2014}, the CNN head applies one-dimensional convolutions along the sequence to detect local n-gram patterns. Before convolution, the padding positions of $\mathbf{H}$ are set to 0. For each kernel size $k \in \{2, 3, 4\}$, there are $F = 128$ filters; filter $j$ produces a feature sequence
\begin{equation}
  c^{(k,j)}_i = \mathrm{ReLU}\!\left(\mathbf{w}^{(k,j)} \cdot \mathbf{H}_{i:i+k-1} + b^{(k,j)}\right).
\end{equation}
Max-over-time pooling then retains the maximum value of each filter across valid windows, i.e., windows that end at a valid token. Concatenating these values yields $\mathbf{z} \in \mathbb{R}^{3F} = \mathbb{R}^{384}$.

\textbf{BiLSTM.} The BiLSTM head~\cite{lstm,bilstm} reads $\mathbf{H}$ in two dimensions so that each position carries both preceding and following context. The sequence is packed to its true length $T$ (packed sequence), so the buffer positions are completely ignored. The representation vector is the concatenation of the final hidden states from both dimensions:
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{z} = [\,\overrightarrow{\mathbf{h}}_T \,;\, \overleftarrow{\mathbf{h}}_1\,] \in \mathbb{R}^{2h},
\end{equation}
where $\overrightarrow{\mathbf{h}}_T$ aggregates information from the beginning to the end of the text, $\overleftarrow{\mathbf{h}}_1$ aggregated from the end to the beginning, and $h = 128$ is the hidden dimension for each dimension (one layer).

\textbf{CNN\,$\|$\,BiLSTM (extended).} To test whether the two classification models complement each other, I added a third head to BamiBERT, consisting of a CNN branch and a BiLSTM branch---as described above---running in parallel on the same $\mathbf{H}$. The two output vectors are concatenated into a single vector $\mathbf{z} \in \mathbb{R}^{640}$ and trained jointly using a single loss function. This head is not part of the four main combinations and is evaluated separately.

\textbf{Output layer and loss function.} For each head, $\mathbf{z}$ passes through Dropout (rate 0.1), a linear layer, and a softmax that outputs the probability of the two classes. The model is trained with the cross-entropy loss.

% =====================================================================
\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\subsection{Data Split}
I used group-stratified 5-fold cross-validation (StratifiedGroupKFold). Each AI-rewritten article was placed in the same group as its original article (matched by URL), so an original article and its rewritten version never end up on opposite sides, one in the training set and the other in the test set; stratifying by text type keeps the proportion of the three types consistent across folds. Each fold uses 20\% of the data as the test set; from the remaining 80\%, a portion equivalent to 10\% of the total data (also divided by group) is set aside as the validation set, so each fold has a training/validation/test ratio of approximately 70/10/20. Each sample appears exactly once in the test set, so out-of-fold predictions cover all 5,000 samples.

\subsection{Implementation Details}
Four models sharing the same training hyperparameters (Table~\ref{tab:hparams}). The models were trained for 30 epochs without early stopping; after at each epoch, the macro-F1 score on the validation set is calculated, and the weights from the epoch with the highest macro-F1 score are retained for evaluation on the test set. The experiments were run on an NVIDIA RTX 2070 GPU (8~GB) using PyTorch 2.7.1 (CUDA 11.8) and Hugging Face Transformers.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Common training hyperparameters for the four models.}
  \label{tab:hparams}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
  \begin{tabular}{ll}
    \toprule
    Hyperparameter & Value \\
    \midrule
    Encoder & frozen \\
    $S$ (number of leading syllables kept) & 225 \\
    $L$ (maximum number of tokens) & PhoBERT 256, BamiBERT 512 \\
    $d$ (encoder hidden size) & 768 \\
    Optimizer & AdamW \cite{adamw} \\
    $\eta$ (learning rate) & $10^{-3}$ \\
    $\lambda$ (weight decay) & 0.01 \\
    $B$ (batch size) & 32 \\
    $E$ (number of epochs) & 30, no early stopping \\
    $\tau$ (gradient-norm clipping threshold) & 1.0 \\
    $p$ (dropout rate) & 0.1 \\
    $k$ (CNN kernel sizes) & 2, 3, 4 \\
    $F$ (CNN filters per $k$) & 128 \\
    $h$ (BiLSTM hidden size per direction) & 128 \\
    BiLSTM layers & 1 \\
    Loss function & cross-entropy \\
    Epoch selection & best validation macro-F1 \\
    $s$ (seed of fold $f$) & $42 + f$ \\
    Numerical precision & mixed (fp16) \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
The AI class is considered the positive class. I use Accuracy, Precision, Recall, and F1 (the harmonic mean of Precision and Recall) for the positive class. The metrics are calculated on the test set of each fold and reported as the mean $\pm$ standard deviation across 5 folds. I also report accuracy separately for each text type and ROC-AUC.

% =====================================================================
\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Overall Performance}
Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the results of the 5-fold cross-validation. BamiBERT + CNN achieved the highest results in Accuracy (95.42\%), Recall (93.44\%), and F1 (95.33\%). PhoBERT + CNN followed closely with an Accuracy of 94.84\% and an F1 of 94.69\%, and had the highest Precision (97.61\% vs. 97.30\%). The standard deviation of BamiBERT + CNN is also the smallest (Accuracy $\pm$0.42 compared to $\pm$0.76 for PhoBERT + CNN), indicating that the model is more stable when the training data changes.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Results of 5-fold cross-validation (\%, mean $\pm$ standard deviation). Best values in bold.}
  \label{tab:main}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{0.8pt}
  \begin{tabular}{lcccc}
    \toprule
    Model & Accuracy & Precision & Recall & F1 \\
    \midrule
    PhoBERT + CNN     & \pmv{94.84}{0.76} & \pmv{\textbf{97.61}}{1.70} & \pmv{91.96}{1.11} & \pmv{94.69}{0.75} \\
    PhoBERT + BiLSTM  & \pmv{90.60}{0.58} & \pmv{94.13}{1.59} & \pmv{86.64}{1.74} & \pmv{90.21}{0.64} \\
    BamiBERT + CNN    & \pmv{\textbf{95.42}}{0.42} & \pmv{97.30}{0.98} & \pmv{\textbf{93.44}}{0.48} & \pmv{\textbf{95.33}}{0.40} \\
    BamiBERT + BiLSTM & \pmv{91.30}{0.83} & \pmv{93.73}{1.44} & \pmv{88.56}{2.08} & \pmv{91.05}{0.93} \\
    \midrule
    {\scriptsize BamiBERT + CNN$\|$BiLSTM} & \pmv{95.02}{0.35} & \pmv{96.58}{1.07} & \pmv{93.36}{0.68} & \pmv{94.94}{0.33} \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The impact of the classification model is significantly greater than that of the encoder. For both encoders, the CNN outperforms the BiLSTM by approximately 4 percentage points in accuracy (PhoBERT: 94.84\% vs. 90.60\%; BamiBERT: 95.42\% vs. 91.30\%). Conversely, when keeping the classification model constant, replacing PhoBERT with BamiBERT only increased Accuracy by 0.58 points (CNN) and 0.70 points (BiLSTM). This increase is small compared to the standard deviation across folds (0.42--0.83 points), so it is not sufficient to conclusively state that BamiBERT is better than PhoBERT; conversely, the approximately 4-point difference between CNN and BiLSTM is much larger than the standard deviation.

With the combined CNN$\|$BiLSTM head (the last row of Table~\ref{tab:main}), I ran the same 5-fold cross-validation using the exact same folds and seeds as above. This head does not outperform BamiBERT + CNN: Accuracy of 95.02\% versus 95.42\%, a difference of less than 0.4 points and within the standard deviation across folds. It outperforms both BiLSTM models by about 4 points. Thus, combining the two heads only brings the model up to the level of the standalone CNN without surpassing it.

\subsection{Performance by Text Type}
Table~\ref{tab:bytype} breaks down accuracy by three text types. The AI-Generated group has nearly reached saturation (99.36\% for both CNN models), and the Human group shows comparable accuracy between the two encoders (97.72\% versus 97.40\% for CNN; PhoBERT performs slightly better, consistent with its higher Precision in Table~\ref{tab:main}). The gap between the two encoders lies almost entirely in the AI-Rewritten group, the most challenging category: BamiBERT gains 2.96 points over PhoBERT with the CNN head (87.52\% vs. 84.56\%) and 3.92 points with the BiLSTM head (79.20\% vs. 75.28\%). This is the source of the higher Recall in BamiBERT. The ROC-AUC of the two encoders is nearly identical (98.94\% vs. 98.87\% with CNN), meaning their ability to rank AI-generated text higher than human-generated text is comparable; the difference is primarily reflected in the accuracy at the decision threshold of 0.5.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Accuracy by text type and ROC-AUC (\%, mean $\pm$ standard deviation over 5 folds).}
  \label{tab:bytype}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{0.8pt}
  \begin{tabular}{lcccc}
    \toprule
    Model & Human & AI-Gen. & AI-Rewr. & ROC-AUC \\
    \midrule
    PhoBERT + CNN     & \pmv{\textbf{97.72}}{1.69} & \pmv{\textbf{99.36}}{0.61} & \pmv{84.56}{2.72} & \pmv{98.87}{0.15} \\
    PhoBERT + BiLSTM  & \pmv{94.56}{1.62} & \pmv{98.00}{0.80} & \pmv{75.28}{3.46} & \pmv{96.57}{0.41} \\
    BamiBERT + CNN    & \pmv{97.40}{0.97} & \pmv{\textbf{99.36}}{0.78} & \pmv{\textbf{87.52}}{1.31} & \pmv{\textbf{98.94}}{0.16} \\
    BamiBERT + BiLSTM & \pmv{94.04}{1.54} & \pmv{97.92}{1.25} & \pmv{79.20}{3.14} & \pmv{96.89}{0.42} \\
    \midrule
    {\scriptsize BamiBERT + CNN$\|$BiLSTM} & \pmv{96.68}{1.08} & \pmv{99.28}{0.77} & \pmv{87.44}{1.28} & \pmv{98.84}{0.21} \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Error Analysis}
Figure~\ref{fig:scores} shows the probability distribution of AI classifications assigned by BamiBERT + CNN to each text type (out-of-fold predictions, y-axis on a logarithmic scale, dashed line representing the decision threshold of 0.5). Human text clusters near 0 and AI-generated text clusters near 1, so the model makes very few errors with these two types (65 and 8 texts, respectively). AI-rewritten text has two peaks: most are near 1, but 109 out of 156 incorrectly classified texts have an AI probability below 0.1, meaning the model is confident they were written by humans. Overall, 137 out of 229 errors (59.8\%) had a probability of 0.9 or higher on the incorrect side, so the errors are primarily confidence errors rather than cases where the threshold was narrowly missed. This aligns with the fact that validation loss increased while accuracy did not decrease (see the loss curve) and indicates that adjusting the decision threshold is unlikely to resolve these errors.

Figure~\ref{fig:overlap} counts the number of texts where only one of the two encoders predicted correctly when using the CNN head. BamiBERT's advantage stems almost entirely from the AI-Rewritten group (57 texts correctly predicted only by BamiBERT versus 20 correctly predicted only by PhoBERT), while in the Human group, BamiBERT performs slightly worse (31 versus 39), and in the AI-Generated group, the two encoders are virtually indistinguishable (2 versus 2). In total, there were 90 texts where only BamiBERT was correct and 61 texts where only PhoBERT was correct, meaning the two encoders disagreed on very few samples (151 out of 5,000). Furthermore, 136 out of 156 AI-rewritten texts that BamiBERT misclassified were also misclassified by PhoBERT, so most of the errors were common to both encoders; the limitation may lie in this type of text itself rather than in the choice of encoder.

