  \label{fig:loss}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Computational Cost}
Table~\ref{tab:time} reports the training and test time per fold. Since the encoders are frozen, the features of each encoder are extracted only once for the 5,000 texts and shared across the CNN, BiLSTM, and all folds; this one-time cost is not in the table. BamiBERT is approximately 2.6 times slower than PhoBERT during the feature extraction step (104.97~s vs. 40.95~s), 2.1 times slower during CNN training, and 1.5 times slower during BiLSTM training (Table~\ref{tab:time}). Testing on one fold takes only 0.58--1.29~s for all four models. The main reason is that, with the same 225 syllables, BamiBERT's tokenizer generates longer token sequences, increasing the computational cost of both the encoder and the classification model. The best epoch selected was similar on average across the 5 folds (between 11.2 and 13.4), so the time difference is not due to the number of epochs. In summary, compared to PhoBERT + CNN, BamiBERT + CNN achieves a 0.58-point increase in Accuracy and a 1.48-point increase in Recall but takes approximately twice as long. The head combining CNN$\|$BiLSTM on BamiBERT took approximately 260--360~s per fold in the first four folds, which is longer than CNN (201.56~s) and BiLSTM (206.25~s); the fifth fold took 1,093~s because the machine was running another task, so its average time is not used for comparison.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Training and test time (seconds) per fold on an RTX 2070, averaged over 5 folds.}
  \label{tab:time}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{6pt}
  \begin{tabular}{lcc}
    \toprule
    Model & Train (s) & Test (s) \\
    \midrule
    PhoBERT + CNN     &   94.65 & 0.58 \\
    PhoBERT + BiLSTM  &  133.53 & 0.78 \\
    BamiBERT + CNN    &  201.56 & 1.16 \\
    BamiBERT + BiLSTM &  206.25 & 1.29 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Loss Curves}
Figure~\ref{fig:loss} shows the cross-entropy loss over epochs, averaged across 5 folds. The training loss for all four models converges to near 0 after approximately 9--12 epochs. The loss on the validation and test sets does not follow the same trend: for the CNN, the loss reaches its lowest point around epochs 2--3, then rises slowly and stabilizes around 0.2; for the BiLSTM, the loss increases continuously from approximately 0.22 to 0.45--0.55 and fluctuates more significantly across the folds. The two encoders exhibit nearly identical loss curves when using the same classification model, indicating the phenomenon of overfitting primarily stems from the classification model rather than the encoder. This is why I retained the weights from the epoch with the highest macro-F1 validation score instead of the final epoch. The test set shown in the figure is for observation only and is not used in model selection.



% =====================================================================
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\textbf{BamiBERT vs. PhoBERT.} When both encoders process the same content, BamiBERT outperforms PhoBERT, but by a narrow margin: the difference is only about 0.6--0.7 points in Accuracy, which is small compared to the standard deviation across folds (0.4--0.8 points); therefore, additional data or multiple runs with different seeds are needed to confirm this result. BamiBERT's advantage is concentrated in the AI-Rewritten group, where the content remains the author's own and only the phrasing has changed. One plausible explanation is that BamiBERT represents raw text at the subword level, thus preserving details regarding word choice and punctuation that PhoBERT's tokenization step might lose; however, this hypothesis requires further validation.

\textbf{CNN vs. BiLSTM.} CNN outperforms BiLSTM with both encoders and exhibits fewer signs of overfitting. Since the encoder's hidden state already carries bidirectional context, adding sequential modeling via BiLSTM offers little benefit, while BiLSTM has more parameters and is prone to overfitting on approximately 3,600 training samples per fold. In contrast, a CNN with max-pooling only needs to detect a few characteristic local patterns at any position, which aligns well with the discriminative signals of AI-generated text, which are often manifested through phrases and local expressions.

\textbf{Combining CNN and BiLSTM.} The combined model achieved 95.02\% accuracy, comparable to the standalone CNN (95.42\%, 0.4 percentage points lower and within the standard deviation across folds), and showed no improvement on the hardest dataset (AI-Rewritten: 87.44\% vs. 87.52\%). An initial 70/15/15 split yielded slightly better results than the CNN (95.04\% vs. 94.39\% on 766 samples), but that difference disappeared during cross-validation, indicating that a simple split can lead to misleading conclusions. The BiLSTM branch did not provide useful additional information to the CNN on frozen features and also increased training costs.

\textbf{Limitations.} This study has several limitations. First, the data comes from only one domain (IT news), and the AI-generated text is sourced from only two LLMs (Gemini and DeepSeek, free versions), so the ability to generalize to other domains and to other LLMs such as ChatGPT or Claude has not been evaluated. Second, the encoder was used only in its frozen form; fine-tuning the entire model might alter the rankings. Third, hyperparameters were kept uniform and thus not optimized individually for each model, and each fold ran only one seed. Finally, the model read only the first 225 syllables of each article.

% =====================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
% NOTE: in the PDF this section is titled "DISCUSSION" by mistake; the heading here stays "Conclusion".
This paper presents a controlled comparison between PhoBERT and BamiBERT as frozen feature extractors for the task of detecting AI-generated Vietnamese text, alongside two classification models: CNN and BiLSTM. On a dataset of 5,000 IT news articles, with both encoders reading the first 225 syllables of each article, BamiBERT + CNN achieved the best results (Accuracy 95.42\%, F1 95.33\%). BamiBERT outperformed PhoBERT by approximately 0.6 percentage points in accuracy when using a CNN, with its advantage concentrated in AI-rewritten text, but at roughly double the computational cost. The choice of classification model has a greater impact on the results than the choice of encoder: CNN significantly outperforms BiLSTM with both encoders, while combining CNN and BiLSTM in parallel does not improve upon the single CNN (95.02\% vs. 95.42\%).

In the future, I plan to fine-tune the entire encoder, expand the dataset to include multiple domains and various LLMs, evaluate the model on texts longer than 225 syllables, and analyze the signals the model uses to identify AI-rewritten text.

